A deep link is untrusted input the moment it arrives — it can come from another app, a browser, a QR code, or a malicious actor who crafted it specifically to reach a screen your app didn't expect it to reach with attacker-controlled parameters.

```kotlin
fun handleDeepLink(uri: Uri) {
    // Never trust the scheme/host blindly — verify against an allowlist
    if (uri.host !in ALLOWED_HOSTS) return

    val userId = uri.getQueryParameter("userId")
    // Never navigate straight to a screen using an ID with no ownership check —
    // the link doesn't prove the current user is allowed to see that resource
    if (userId == null || !isValidId(userId)) return

    navigateTo(ProfileScreen(userId), requireAuthCheck = true)
}
```

Two checks matter beyond basic parsing: **validate the link's structure against an allowlist** (host, path shape) before acting on it, and **re-check authorization server-side** for whatever the link points to — a deep link to `/order/12345` should never bypass the normal "does this user own order 12345" check just because it arrived via a link instead of in-app navigation.
